Pallasite Gallop

Ingredients: PROGRESSIVE TRANCE + THRASH METAL + DARK, SINISTER & GOTHIC + Ukulele + 1980s ERA
Creative Catalyst: Musical machinery forged from extraterrestrial nickel-iron meteorites and 18th-century pendulum clock escapements.

Pallasite Gallop is a breathless collision of 140-BPM progressive trance arpeggiations and frantic, palm-muted thrash metal gallops, punctured by the brittle, hyper-speed attack of steel-strung ukuleles welded to geared celestial iron. Drenched in gated 1980s studio reverbs and mournful liturgical drones, the sound oscillates between gothic cathedral dread and mechanical kinetic fury.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 138–146 BPM; rapid-fire double-bass thrash skanks locked in rigid, interlocking precision with a driving four-on-the-floor trance kick, subdivided by the syncopated 16th-note chatter of antique brass clockwork escapements.
- Harmonic Palette: Phrygian dominant and Locrian modes; baroque minor-key sequences, dissonant tritone stabs, and sinister descending chromatic patterns transposed onto hyper-extended high-register string runs.
- Production Signatures: Gated AMS RMX16 non-linear reverb tails on explosive snares; sweeping analog 24dB low-pass filter passes applied across walls of high-gain guitar distortion; mechanical flutter and tactile mechanical clicks harvested from contact mics clamped directly onto antique escapement wheels.
- Dynamics: Ten-minute hypnotic ascents constructed from layered tremolo riffs and glittering ukulele ostinatos that swell into apocalyptic thrash climaxes, followed by sudden "deadfall" drops where all rhythm cuts out, leaving only hollow church resonance and winding springs before restarting the assault.

Scene & Origin Lore
The movement ignited in the winter of 1987 along the snowbound Bavarian-Bohemian border, inside an abandoned glass-annealing plant adjacent to the St. Jude horological monastery. Disillusioned speed-metal guitarists and underground gothic synth producers converged after a pallasite meteorite plummeted through the glass factory’s vaulted leaded roof, embedding extraterrestrial iron directly into the abbey's antique clock towers. The scavengers chiseled out the meteorite chunks, mounting them as soundboards and bridge plates on four-string soprano ukuleles, linking the instrument bodies directly to wind-up escapement assemblies to achieve impossible, inhuman picking speeds. The subculture—calling themselves Uhr-Teufel (Clock-Devils)—congregated in cavernous, unheated cullet cellars lit exclusively by hand-blown cobalt lanterns. Their dress merged 1980s post-punk mourning frock coats with torn thrash battle vests, blackened copper clockmaker monocles, and gauntlets lined with exposed whirring gears, dancing in rigid, pendular headbanging rituals while consuming absinthe laced with crushed iron filings.

Central Instruments
- The Pallasite Ukulele: Four-string acoustic instruments featuring carved meteorite soundboards and piano-wire strings, plucked with brass gear teeth to produce a deafening, chiming metallic rasp.
- Clockwork Thrash Guitars: 1980s modified Jackson Rhoads and Gibson Flying V guitars fitted with spring-loaded pendulum balances that mechanically vibrate the pickups for fluttering sub-harmonic resonance.
- The Escapement Rig: An acoustic drum kit hybridized with Simmons SDSV electronic pads, an iron-rimmed 18-inch kick drum, and mic'd grandfather clock escapements that govern the rhythm.
- Vintage Goth Synths: Roland Juno-106, Fairlight CMI, and PPG Wave 2.2 running icy arpeggios through cranked Marshall JCM800 tube stacks to simulate demonic pipe organs.

